      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "A 34-year-old gravid woman with a history of ileal Crohn's disease presented at 30 weeks' gestation with a 2-week history of fever and right upper quadrant pain. An intra-abdominal abscess was suspected. Ultrasound and MRI failed to demonstrate the suspected abscess. Owing to ongoing pain and fever, the risk to the fetus of a CT scan were discussed with the patient, obstetricians and radiologists, with considerable debate about the possibility of other explanations for her symptoms. Ultimately, a CT scan revealed marked thickening of the distal ileum and confirmed diagnosis of an abscess in continuity with the inflamed bowel."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INVESTIGATIONS",
          "content": "Ultrasound, MRI, CT scan, urinalysis, urine culture and liver function tests."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "DIAGNOSIS",
          "content": "Crohn's disease flare complicated by an intra-abdominal abscess."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "MANAGEMENT",
          "content": "Antibiotics (ceftizoxime, metronidazole and amoxicillin/clavulanate potassium), parenteral nutrition and ileocecectomy."
        }
      ]},
